  "body": "Simple question, I hope: does `<html lang=\"\">` (HTML element with empty string as `lang` attribute value) produce same outcome (wrt document language assignment) as serving document with no `lang` attribute nor `content-language` HTTP header nor its `meta` equivalent?\r\n\r\nIt sounds intuitive, yet I was unable to find it explicitly stated anywhere.  (I'm sorry if it just my mistake.)\r\n\r\nFrom CSS perspective, in https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/6915 it is being clarified that elements with explicit `lang=\"\"` (and descendants) should be matched by `:lang(\"\")` selector and elements with any non-blank language set or inherited should be matched with `:lang(\"*\")`, in the future.\r\n\r\nWhat remains to clarify is whether that empty string is also the \"initial\" language value of elements inside \"unlabelled\" document.  (I assume the XML-y `\"und\"` is not relevant in HTML context any more (?), but really want to be sure and eventually understand the difference from \"blank lang\".)\r\n\r\nIf so, \r\n\r\n```HTML\r\ndata:text/html,<!doctype html>\r\n<html>\r\n<!-- Neither lang attribute, content-language HTTP header, nor equiv meta. -->\r\n<title>?</title>\r\n<style>\r\nbody:lang(\"\")::before {\r\n content: 'X';\r\n}\r\nb:lang(\"*\")::before {\r\n content: 'Y';\r\n}\r\n</style>\r\n<body>-<b lang=\"zz\">-</b>\r\n```\r\n\r\nshould display `X-Y-`, then. (If the linked conclusion makes it into specs.) ",
